{"name":"Mystic Rose","hex":"#f8c0ba","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mystic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, or relating to mystics, mysticism or occult mysteries; mystical.","Mysterious and strange; arcane, obscure or enigmatic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone who practices mysticism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mystic"},{"term":"rose","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shrub of the genus Rosa, with red, pink, white or yellow flowers.","A flower of the rose plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make rose-colored; to redden or flush.","To perfume, as with roses."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rose symbolism","blocks":[{"text":"Various folk cultures and traditions assign symbolic meaning to the rose, though these are seldom understood in-depth. Examples of deeper meanings lie within the language of flowers, and how a rose may have a different meaning in arrangements. Examples of common meanings of different coloured roses are: true love (red), mystery (blue), innocence or purity (white), death (black), friendship (yellow), and passion (orange)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_symbolism","wikidata":"Q3941237"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose","wikidata":"Q34687"},{"title":"Rose (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Rose is the color halfway between red and magenta, whose hue corresponds to an angle of 330 degrees on the HSV/HSL color wheel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg/330px-Pink-Rose-Sitia-Crete.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_(color)","wikidata":"Q533047"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Johannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hans Simon Holtzbecker: Rosa gallica, gouache, c. 1650 (Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen).The vivid red, semi-double Rosa gallica was \"the ancestor of all the roses of medieval Europe\".[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohannes_Simon_Holtzbecher_-_Rosa_gallica_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common